  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nThe weather pattern through at least the middle of the week will \ncontinue to support good moisture levels over at least the \neastern half of Arizona and likely improved upper level ascent for\nTuesday-Thursday. Tuesday may involve a brief period of drier air\nmoving into south-central Arizona from the southwest and this\nshows up in the fairly limited PoPs Tuesday afternoon/evening.\nHowever, NBM guidance shows a resurgence of moisture and rain \nchances during the overnight hours Tuesday night/Wednesday \nmorning. Chances Wednesday into Thursday are also looking better \nwith a potential disturbance tracking northward into \ncentral/eastern Arizona at some point. Needless to say, the model \ndifferences call into question the best timing for rain chances \nand the exact areas, but as long as the upper level support and \ngood moisture remain present chances will remain elevated. \n\nWPC has added a slight risk on the Days 2-4 (Monday- Wednesday) \nExcessive Rainfall Outlooks over the eastern Arizona high \nterrain. It would not be surprising for subsequent issuances of \nthe EROs to extend the risk through Thursday or even Friday \ndepending on the forecast available moisture. Although the risk \nfor heavy rainfall and flooding will be present through at least \nthe middle of the week, the risk for strong downdraft/outflow \nwinds is likely to decrease with guidance showing decreasing \nforecast DCAPEs. Models are still trending toward drier air \nfinally moving eastward through most of Arizona by Friday or \nSaturday. This should mostly shut down rain chances over the lower\ndeserts and decrease chances over higher terrain areas.\n\nTemperatures throughout the entire week are expected to remain\nfairly close to seasonal normals with the lowest forecast highs\noccurring on Tuesday and Wednesday. As long as models are correct\nwith the drying trend late in the week into next weekend,\ntemperatures are favored to drift back to above normal with highs\nmore in a 103-108 degree range by Friday or Saturday.",
